    crm:P129i_is_subject_of <https://mbdiaries.itatti.harvard.edu/diary/1903-04/document/165> ;
    crm:P183i_has_representation <https://ids.lib.harvard.edu/ids/iiif/493312435/full/full/0/default.jpg> ;
    rdf:value "centre. I lunched with Cousin Carrie Lawrence and her family and the Everetts - a dull, longdrawn out, middle-class lunch, ungarnished with conversation. Then they trailed me a mile through the mud to “the most beautiful building in the United States”, the Art and Science Building of the Pennsylvania University, where I was to lecture. This kind of buildings turned out to be a horrible be- mosaic-ed romanesque structure of disagreeably coloured brick, it looked thoroughly German, and of course horrible. The organization of the lecture was bad, but still the hall got packed, and I spoke for an hour. Miss Rapplier introduced me. I felt it up-hill work, because the audience was peppered with faces of cousins and uncles and relatives of various degrees of kinship, who I  knew  cared absolutely nothing about "^^xsd:string ;
    mbdiaries-ontology:index 165 ;
    mbdiaries-ontology:part_of <https://mbdiaries.itatti.harvard.edu/resource/diary/1903-04> .

